# Contrast test for a NON-streaming buffer behind an LTE hook. The bound is the
# state where the buffer completes: http.header.raw's engine is registered at
# HEADERS, and a firewall rule is only evaluated at its hook, so a bound above
# HEADERS no longer loads. Two POST flows:
#
# * flow A (port 49165): request headers contain "X-Marker: yes" (keyword
#   present);
# * flow B (port 49166): request headers contain "X-Marker: no" (keyword
#   absent).
#
# Unlike the streaming buffers (tests 233-238), the no match of flow B at the
# rule's own hook state is DEFINITIVE (the buffer cannot receive more data),
# so the LTE no-match -> CANT_MATCH conversion fires at the first body pass
# and the flow drops by the default app policy there - the drop lands one
# state EARLIER than in the streaming case (test 235), where the decision is
# deferred to the body-completion pass.
#
# * flow A: the final buffer matches at the first body pass and the
#   accept:flow accepts the flow.
#
# Expected: exactly 1 `firewall default app policy` drop (flow B).
#
# accept:hook tcp:all $HOME_NET any <> $EXTERNAL_NET any (flow:not_established; sid:1021;)
# accept:hook tcp:all $HOME_NET any <> $EXTERNAL_NET any (flow:established; sid:1022;)

# allow session setup
accept:hook tcp:all $HOME_NET any <> $EXTERNAL_NET any (flow:not_established; sid:1021;)
accept:hook tcp:all $HOME_NET any <> $EXTERNAL_NET any (flow:established; sid:1022;)

accept:flow,alert http1:<request_headers $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (http.header.raw; content:"X-Marker: yes"; sid:211;)
